  "limitations": [
    "The draft public centerline measures 775.2 m. The Paris Musées planning trace measures about 785 m when fitted to the two public portals and former Fontis address. The museum lists a 1.5 km visit. The difference remains unresolved.",
    "Passage and stair geometry use visual estimates. The lower quarry floor is modeled 1.65 m below the upper level.",
    "The draft shaft centers move inside the mapped portal footprints. The upper landings meet the registered entrance and exit floor elevations. This fits the study to the street controls. The exact shafts and visitor rooms remain inferred.",
    "The public stairs now connect to the modeled upper and lower landings. The two street portals have independent placements. The complete underground alignment and native terrain integration remain open.",
    "The September Martyrs’ wall is a restoration-photo reconstruction. Its location in the gallery trace and the smaller tablet letterforms are approximate.",
    "The visitor plan sets the relative position of the Lamp Crypt between the Samaritaine and later memorial galleries. The model uses a widened bend at the approximate stop. Its actual chamber footprint, measured dimensions and bearings are unresolved.",
    "The Port-Mahon branch uses the 2025 museum diagram and photographs. The relative layout, gallery height, tread count and well depth are reconstructed. The branch is a separately identified guided tour section. The exact underground alignment remains pending.",
    "The three Décure reliefs follow published photographs and the Port-Mahon branch shown on the 2025 museum plan. Alcove spacing, depth, side of the gallery and the exact relief dimensions remain estimated.",
    "The museum room plan documents 83 spiral steps from the Fontis vestibule toward the former exit at 36 rue Rémy-Dumoncel. The draft takes the 83-step spiral to the sampled ground elevation behind the photographed street frontage. The event gallery rises about 7 m across its 247 m modeled length, a visualization estimate consistent with an approximately 18 m depth at the Fontis end. The shaft center, grade profile and surface connection beyond the upper landing remain estimated.",
    "The renovated Pillars Hall furnishings and ossuary vestibule stonework follow the museum’s April 2026 photos. The walking surface, stairs and room shapes follow the reconstructed plan. Their exact dimensions, underground locations and exhibit graphics remain estimated.",
    "The renovated hague follows stop 8 in the museum visitor guide, before the Samaritaine well. Its modeled wall side, length and position in the draft gallery are estimates.",
    "The museum’s stop 7 photograph establishes the plaque profile and bone arrangement. The visitor guide orders it after the ossuary gate and before the renovated hague. Its wall side, dimensions and position in the gallery are estimates.",
    "The Tuileries memorial follows the museum photograph at stop 10. The draft places it after the September Martyrs wall and before the Crypt of the Passion. Its dimensions and wall position are estimated.",
    "The Sacellum uses the museum photograph for the stepped altar and the arrangement of the three tablets. Its chamber, wall side and distances are estimated between the Samaritaine and the lamp. Small lettering is unresolved.",
    "The April 2026 photograph shows the HAUTEUR depth inscription. Its position along the entry gallery and the dimensions of its stone panel are estimated.",
    "The architect’s cutaway establishes the side of the passage and the direction of the upper landing. The shaft center, diameter and room dimensions are estimates within the PMTiles footprint.",
    "The museum plan shows an additional east ossuary leg between the northern junction and the Crypt of the Passion approach. Its size, exact bearings and current direction of visitor travel remain estimated.",
    "The museum photograph records the incised Rue Hallé plaque in a quarry reinforcement wall. The model places it in the lower approach near the PMTiles street crossing. The actual passage location, wall side and plaque dimensions remain estimated.",
    "The Denfert stair approach uses a short enclosed room and open doorway, based on the architect’s cutaway. The door, shaft and partition positions within the footprint remain approximate.",
    "The Paris Musées planning diagram marks the bends toward René-Coty and a separate guided branch toward the former Fontis exit. The revised draft follows the exit-side return and joins the Fontis gallery there. Registration uses both public portals and the Fontis address. Exact junction geometry and final as-built underground positions remain estimates."
  ],
